      "locked": false,
      "text": "--> super class reference can point to child class object .\n\n\nWHY DOES JAVA ALLOW PARENT CLASS REFERENCE TO\nPOINT TO CHILD CLASS OBJECT?\n\nSyntax:\nParent p = new Child();\n\nMain Reason:\nThe main reason is to achieve Runtime Polymorphism.\n\n- A Child IS-A Parent because of inheritance.\n- Therefore, a Parent reference can legally point to a\nChild object.\n- This allows the same Parent reference to work with\ndifferent Child objects.\n- When a method is overridden, Java decides at runtime\nwhich method should execute based on the actual object.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\na.sound();\n\nOutput:\nDog barks\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\nReference Type  → Animal\nObject Type     → Dog\n\n- Reference type decides which members can be accessed.\n- Actual object type decides which overridden method\nexecutes.\n\n\nWHY IS THIS USEFUL?\n\nAnimal a;\n\na = new Dog();\na = new Cat();\na = new Horse();\n\nThe same Animal reference can work with different child\nobjects.\n\nAdvantages:\n1. Runtime Polymorphism\n2. Flexibility\n3. Code Reusability\n4. Loose Coupling\n5. Easier Maintenance and Extension\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Java allows a parent class reference to point to a child\nclass object because a child IS-A parent. This enables\nruntime polymorphism, where the same parent reference\ncan work with different child objects and the overridden\nmethod is selected at runtime based on the actual\nobject.\"WHY DOES JAVA ALLOW PARENT CLASS\nREFERENCE TO POINT TO CHILD CLASS OBJECT?\n\nSyntax:\nParent p = new Child();\n\nMain Reason:\nThe main reason is to achieve Runtime Polymorphism.\n\n- A Child IS-A Parent because of inheritance.\n- Therefore, a Parent reference can legally point to a\nChild object.\n- This allows the same Parent reference to work with\ndifferent Child objects.\n- When a method is overridden, Java decides at runtime\nwhich method should execute based on the actual object.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\na.sound();\n\nOutput:\nDog barks\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\nReference Type  → Animal\nObject Type     → Dog\n\n- Reference type decides which members can be accessed.\n- Actual object type decides which overridden method\nexecutes.\n\n\nWHY IS THIS USEFUL?\n\nAnimal a;\n\na = new Dog();\na = new Cat();\na = new Horse();\n\nThe same Animal reference can work with different child\nobjects.\n\nAdvantages:\n1. Runtime Polymorphism\n2. Flexibility\n3. Code Reusability\n4. Loose Coupling\n5. Easier Maintenance and Extension\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Java allows a parent class reference to point to a child\nclass object because a child IS-A parent. This enables\nruntime polymorphism, where the same parent reference\ncan work with different child objects and the overridden\nmethod is selected at runtime based on the actual\nobject.\"WHY DOES JAVA ALLOW PARENT CLASS\nREFERENCE TO POINT TO CHILD CLASS OBJECT?\n\nSyntax:\nParent p = new Child();\n\nMain Reason:\nThe main reason is to achieve Runtime Polymorphism.\n\n- A Child IS-A Parent because of inheritance.\n- Therefore, a Parent reference can legally point to a\nChild object.\n- This allows the same Parent reference to work with\ndifferent Child objects.\n- When a method is overridden, Java decides at runtime\nwhich method should execute based on the actual object.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\na.sound();\n\nOutput:\nDog barks\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\nReference Type  → Animal\nObject Type     → Dog\n\n- Reference type decides which members can be accessed.\n- Actual object type decides which overridden method\nexecutes.\n\n\nWHY IS THIS USEFUL?\n\nAnimal a;\n\na = new Dog();\na = new Cat();\na = new Horse();\n\nThe same Animal reference can work with different child\nobjects.\n\nAdvantages:\n1. Runtime Polymorphism\n2. Flexibility\n3. Code Reusability\n4. Loose Coupling\n5. Easier Maintenance and Extension\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Java allows a parent class reference to point to a child\nclass object because a child IS-A parent. This enables\nruntime polymorphism, where the same parent reference\ncan work with different child objects and the overridden\nmethod is selected at runtime based on the actual\nobject.\"\n\n\nRUNTIME POLYMORPHISM – REAL BENEFIT\n\nRuntime polymorphism is useful when we have multiple\nimplementations of the same Parent class / Interface.\n\nMAIN PURPOSE:\n\nThe main purpose of runtime polymorphism is:\n\n\"Write common code once and allow it to work with\ndifferent child implementations.\"\n\nREAL-WORLD EXAMPLE: PAYMENT SYSTEM\n\nSuppose an e-commerce application supports:\n\n- UPI\n- Credit Card\n- Net Banking\n- Wallet\n\n\nWITHOUT POLYMORPHISM:\n\nWe may have to write conditions for every payment type:\n\nvoid makePayment(String type) {\n\n    if(type.equals(\"UPI\")) {\n        // UPI payment logic\n    }\n    else if(type.equals(\"CARD\")) {\n        // Card payment logic\n    }\n    else if(type.equals(\"NETBANKING\")) {\n        // Net banking logic\n    }\n    else if(type.equals(\"WALLET\")) {\n        // Wallet payment logic\n    }\n}\n\nPROBLEM:\n\nWhenever a new payment method is added, we have to\nmodify the existing method.\n\nFor example, if \"Wallet\" is added, we need to modify\nmakePayment().\n\nThis makes the code difficult to maintain and extend.\n\n\nWITH RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM:\n\nCreate a common Parent/Interface:\n\ninterface Payment {\n    void pay();\n}\n\nDifferent implementations:\n\nclass UPIPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing UPI payment\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass CardPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing Card payment\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass NetBankingPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing Net Banking\npayment\");\n    }\n}\n\n\nCOMMON BUSINESS LOGIC:\n\nclass PaymentService {\n\n    void processPayment(Payment payment) {\n        payment.pay();\n    }\n}\n\n\nUSAGE:\n\nPaymentService service = new PaymentService();\n\nservice.processPayment(new UPIPayment());\nservice.processPayment(new CardPayment());\nservice.processPayment(new NetBankingPayment());\n\n\nOUTPUT:\n\nProcessing UPI payment\nProcessing Card payment\nProcessing Net Banking payment\n\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY HAPPENING?\n\nPayment reference\n        ↓\nActual Object\n        ↓\nUPIPayment       → UPIPayment.pay()\nCardPayment      → CardPayment.pay()\nNetBankingPayment → NetBankingPayment.pay()\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nPayment payment = new UPIPayment();\n\nHere:\n\nReference Type → Payment\nObject Type    → UPIPayment\n\nWhen:\n\npayment.pay();\n\nis called, Java checks the actual object at runtime and\nexecutes:\n\nUPIPayment.pay()\n\n\nREAL BENEFIT:\n\nSuppose tomorrow we add a new payment method:\n\nclass WalletPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing Wallet payment\");\n    }\n}\n\nWe can simply write:\n\nservice.processPayment(new WalletPayment());\n\nWe DON'T need to modify PaymentService.\n\nThis is the real benefit of runtime polymorphism.\n\n\nWHY DO WE NEED RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM?\n\n1. Common code can work with different implementations.\n\n2. We don't need separate code for every child class.\n\n3. New implementations can be added easily.\n\n4. Existing common/business logic does not need to\nchange.\n\n5. It provides flexibility and extensibility.\n\n6. It helps achieve loose coupling.\n\n7. It makes large applications easier to maintain.\n\n\nMAIN IDEA:\n\n\"Same parent reference + same method call + different\nbehavior depending on the actual object.\"\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Runtime polymorphism is useful when we have multiple\nimplementations of the same parent type. Instead of\nwriting separate logic for every implementation, we write\ncommon code using the parent reference, and Java decides\nat runtime which overridden method should execute based\non the actual object. This makes the application flexible,\nloosely coupled, and easier to extend.\"\n\n\n\nRULE:\n=====\n\n--> Parent class reference can point to a Child class\nobject.\n\n    Parent p = new Child();       // VALID\n\n\n--> Child class reference cannot directly point to a Parent\nclass object.\n\n    Child c = new Parent();       // INVALID\n\n\nWHY?\n====\n\n--> Because of inheritance, a Child class inherits the\nfeatures of the Parent class.\n\n--> Therefore, a Child object has:\n    1. Its own features\n    2. Inherited features of the Parent\n\n--> But a Parent object has only:\n    1. Its own features\n\n--> A Parent object does NOT have the additional\nfeatures/methods\n    introduced by the Child class.\n\n\nEXAMPLE:\n========\n\nclass Parent {\n    void parentMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void childMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\n\nParent p = new Child();     // VALID\n\nChild c = new Parent();     // INVALID\n\n\nWHY IS Parent p = new Child() VALID?\n\nA Child object contains the Parent part + Child-specific\npart.\n\nSo treating a Child object as a Parent is safe.\n\nWHY IS Child c = new Parent() INVALID?\n\nA Parent object does not necessarily contain the Child-\nspecific part.\n\nIf Java allowed this:\n\nChild c = new Parent();\n\nthen:\n\nc.childMethod();\n\nwould be unsafe because the actual Parent object does\nnot have childMethod().\n\n\nEASY WAY TO REMEMBER:\n=====================\n\nEvery Child IS-A Parent.\n\nBut every Parent is NOT a Child.\n\nTherefore:\n\nParent reference → Child object     ✅\nChild reference  → Parent object     ❌\n\n\n\n--> Parent class reference pointing to child class object\nbut can access/call only those properties/methods which\nare inherited from parent class and cannot access/call\nchild class features .\n\nIMPORTANT:\n==========\n\n--> When a Parent class reference points to a Child class\nobject:\n\n    Parent p = new Child();\n\n\n--> The Parent reference can access/call only those\nmembers\n    that are available in the Parent class.\n\n--> It CANNOT directly access Child-specific members\nthrough\n    the Parent reference.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Parent {\n    void parentMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void childMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\np.parentMethod();    // VALID\np.childMethod();     // INVALID\n\n\nWHY?\n====\n\n--> The reference type is Parent.\n\n--> Therefore, at compile time Java checks the Parent\nclass\n    to determine which members can be accessed.\n\n--> Even though the actual object is Child, the Parent\nreference\n    cannot directly access Child-specific members.\n\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION:\n======================\n\nReference Type → Decides what members can be\naccessed.\n\nActual Object Type → Decides which overridden instance\nmethod\n                      will execute at runtime.\n\n\nExample:\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\np.parentMethod();    // Can access\np.childMethod();     // Cannot access\n\nIf parentMethod() is overridden in Child:\n\np.parentMethod();\n\n→ Child's overridden version will execute at runtime.\n\n\nREMEMBER:\n=========\n\nParent reference → Child object\n\n        ↓\n\nCan access Parent-level members\n        +\nOverridden methods execute according to actual Child\nobject\n\n        ↓\n\nCannot directly access Child-specific members.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "--> super class reference can point to child class object .\n\n\nWHY DOES JAVA ALLOW PARENT CLASS REFERENCE TO POINT TO CHILD CLASS OBJECT?\n\nSyntax:\nParent p = new Child();\n\nMain Reason:\nThe main reason is to achieve Runtime Polymorphism.\n\n- A Child IS-A Parent because of inheritance.\n- Therefore, a Parent reference can legally point to a Child object.\n- This allows the same Parent reference to work with different Child objects.\n- When a method is overridden, Java decides at runtime which method should execute based on the actual object.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\na.sound();\n\nOutput:\nDog barks\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\nReference Type  → Animal\nObject Type     → Dog\n\n- Reference type decides which members can be accessed.\n- Actual object type decides which overridden method executes.\n\n\nWHY IS THIS USEFUL?\n\nAnimal a;\n\na = new Dog();\na = new Cat();\na = new Horse();\n\nThe same Animal reference can work with different child objects.\n\nAdvantages:\n1. Runtime Polymorphism\n2. Flexibility\n3. Code Reusability\n4. Loose Coupling\n5. Easier Maintenance and Extension\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Java allows a parent class reference to point to a child class object because a child IS-A parent. This enables runtime polymorphism, where the same parent reference can work with different child objects and the overridden method is selected at runtime based on the actual object.\"WHY DOES JAVA ALLOW PARENT CLASS REFERENCE TO POINT TO CHILD CLASS OBJECT?\n\nSyntax:\nParent p = new Child();\n\nMain Reason:\nThe main reason is to achieve Runtime Polymorphism.\n\n- A Child IS-A Parent because of inheritance.\n- Therefore, a Parent reference can legally point to a Child object.\n- This allows the same Parent reference to work with different Child objects.\n- When a method is overridden, Java decides at runtime which method should execute based on the actual object.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\na.sound();\n\nOutput:\nDog barks\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\nReference Type  → Animal\nObject Type     → Dog\n\n- Reference type decides which members can be accessed.\n- Actual object type decides which overridden method executes.\n\n\nWHY IS THIS USEFUL?\n\nAnimal a;\n\na = new Dog();\na = new Cat();\na = new Horse();\n\nThe same Animal reference can work with different child objects.\n\nAdvantages:\n1. Runtime Polymorphism\n2. Flexibility\n3. Code Reusability\n4. Loose Coupling\n5. Easier Maintenance and Extension\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Java allows a parent class reference to point to a child class object because a child IS-A parent. This enables runtime polymorphism, where the same parent reference can work with different child objects and the overridden method is selected at runtime based on the actual object.\"WHY DOES JAVA ALLOW PARENT CLASS REFERENCE TO POINT TO CHILD CLASS OBJECT?\n\nSyntax:\nParent p = new Child();\n\nMain Reason:\nThe main reason is to achieve Runtime Polymorphism.\n\n- A Child IS-A Parent because of inheritance.\n- Therefore, a Parent reference can legally point to a Child object.\n- This allows the same Parent reference to work with different Child objects.\n- When a method is overridden, Java decides at runtime which method should execute based on the actual object.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\na.sound();\n\nOutput:\nDog barks\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\n\nReference Type  → Animal\nObject Type     → Dog\n\n- Reference type decides which members can be accessed.\n- Actual object type decides which overridden method executes.\n\n\nWHY IS THIS USEFUL?\n\nAnimal a;\n\na = new Dog();\na = new Cat();\na = new Horse();\n\nThe same Animal reference can work with different child objects.\n\nAdvantages:\n1. Runtime Polymorphism\n2. Flexibility\n3. Code Reusability\n4. Loose Coupling\n5. Easier Maintenance and Extension\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Java allows a parent class reference to point to a child class object because a child IS-A parent. This enables runtime polymorphism, where the same parent reference can work with different child objects and the overridden method is selected at runtime based on the actual object.\"\n\n\nRUNTIME POLYMORPHISM – REAL BENEFIT\n\nRuntime polymorphism is useful when we have multiple implementations of the same Parent class / Interface.\n\nMAIN PURPOSE:\n\nThe main purpose of runtime polymorphism is:\n\n\"Write common code once and allow it to work with different child implementations.\"\n\nREAL-WORLD EXAMPLE: PAYMENT SYSTEM\n\nSuppose an e-commerce application supports:\n\n- UPI\n- Credit Card\n- Net Banking\n- Wallet\n\n\nWITHOUT POLYMORPHISM:\n\nWe may have to write conditions for every payment type:\n\nvoid makePayment(String type) {\n\n    if(type.equals(\"UPI\")) {\n        // UPI payment logic\n    }\n    else if(type.equals(\"CARD\")) {\n        // Card payment logic\n    }\n    else if(type.equals(\"NETBANKING\")) {\n        // Net banking logic\n    }\n    else if(type.equals(\"WALLET\")) {\n        // Wallet payment logic\n    }\n}\n\nPROBLEM:\n\nWhenever a new payment method is added, we have to modify the existing method.\n\nFor example, if \"Wallet\" is added, we need to modify makePayment().\n\nThis makes the code difficult to maintain and extend.\n\n\nWITH RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM:\n\nCreate a common Parent/Interface:\n\ninterface Payment {\n    void pay();\n}\n\nDifferent implementations:\n\nclass UPIPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing UPI payment\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass CardPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing Card payment\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass NetBankingPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing Net Banking payment\");\n    }\n}\n\n\nCOMMON BUSINESS LOGIC:\n\nclass PaymentService {\n\n    void processPayment(Payment payment) {\n        payment.pay();\n    }\n}\n\n\nUSAGE:\n\nPaymentService service = new PaymentService();\n\nservice.processPayment(new UPIPayment());\nservice.processPayment(new CardPayment());\nservice.processPayment(new NetBankingPayment());\n\n\nOUTPUT:\n\nProcessing UPI payment\nProcessing Card payment\nProcessing Net Banking payment\n\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY HAPPENING?\n\nPayment reference\n        ↓\nActual Object\n        ↓\nUPIPayment       → UPIPayment.pay()\nCardPayment      → CardPayment.pay()\nNetBankingPayment → NetBankingPayment.pay()\n\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nPayment payment = new UPIPayment();\n\nHere:\n\nReference Type → Payment\nObject Type    → UPIPayment\n\nWhen:\n\npayment.pay();\n\nis called, Java checks the actual object at runtime and executes:\n\nUPIPayment.pay()\n\n\nREAL BENEFIT:\n\nSuppose tomorrow we add a new payment method:\n\nclass WalletPayment implements Payment {\n\n    public void pay() {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing Wallet payment\");\n    }\n}\n\nWe can simply write:\n\nservice.processPayment(new WalletPayment());\n\nWe DON'T need to modify PaymentService.\n\nThis is the real benefit of runtime polymorphism.\n\n\nWHY DO WE NEED RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM?\n\n1. Common code can work with different implementations.\n\n2. We don't need separate code for every child class.\n\n3. New implementations can be added easily.\n\n4. Existing common/business logic does not need to change.\n\n5. It provides flexibility and extensibility.\n\n6. It helps achieve loose coupling.\n\n7. It makes large applications easier to maintain.\n\n\nMAIN IDEA:\n\n\"Same parent reference + same method call + different behavior depending on the actual object.\"\n\n\nINTERVIEW ANSWER:\n\n\"Runtime polymorphism is useful when we have multiple implementations of the same parent type. Instead of writing separate logic for every implementation, we write common code using the parent reference, and Java decides at runtime which overridden method should execute based on the actual object. This makes the application flexible, loosely coupled, and easier to extend.\"\n\n\n\nRULE:\n=====\n\n--> Parent class reference can point to a Child class object.\n\n    Parent p = new Child();       // VALID\n\n\n--> Child class reference cannot directly point to a Parent class object.\n\n    Child c = new Parent();       // INVALID\n\n\nWHY?\n====\n\n--> Because of inheritance, a Child class inherits the features of the Parent class.\n\n--> Therefore, a Child object has:\n    1. Its own features\n    2. Inherited features of the Parent\n\n--> But a Parent object has only:\n    1. Its own features\n\n--> A Parent object does NOT have the additional features/methods\n    introduced by the Child class.\n\n\nEXAMPLE:\n========\n\nclass Parent {\n    void parentMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void childMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\n\nParent p = new Child();     // VALID\n\nChild c = new Parent();     // INVALID\n\n\nWHY IS Parent p = new Child() VALID?\n\nA Child object contains the Parent part + Child-specific part.\n\nSo treating a Child object as a Parent is safe.\n\nWHY IS Child c = new Parent() INVALID?\n\nA Parent object does not necessarily contain the Child-specific part.\n\nIf Java allowed this:\n\nChild c = new Parent();\n\nthen:\n\nc.childMethod();\n\nwould be unsafe because the actual Parent object does not have childMethod().\n\n\nEASY WAY TO REMEMBER:\n=====================\n\nEvery Child IS-A Parent.\n\nBut every Parent is NOT a Child.\n\nTherefore:\n\nParent reference → Child object     ✅\nChild reference  → Parent object     ❌\n\n\n\n--> Parent class reference pointing to child class object but can access/call only those properties/methods which are inherited from parent class and cannot access/call child class features .\n\nIMPORTANT:\n==========\n\n--> When a Parent class reference points to a Child class object:\n\n    Parent p = new Child();\n\n\n--> The Parent reference can access/call only those members\n    that are available in the Parent class.\n\n--> It CANNOT directly access Child-specific members through\n    the Parent reference.\n\nExample:\n\nclass Parent {\n    void parentMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void childMethod() {\n    }\n}\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\np.parentMethod();    // VALID\np.childMethod();     // INVALID\n\n\nWHY?\n====\n\n--> The reference type is Parent.\n\n--> Therefore, at compile time Java checks the Parent class\n    to determine which members can be accessed.\n\n--> Even though the actual object is Child, the Parent reference\n    cannot directly access Child-specific members.\n\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION:\n======================\n\nReference Type → Decides what members can be accessed.\n\nActual Object Type → Decides which overridden instance method\n                      will execute at runtime.\n\n\nExample:\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\np.parentMethod();    // Can access\np.childMethod();     // Cannot access\n\nIf parentMethod() is overridden in Child:\n\np.parentMethod();\n\n→ Child's overridden version will execute at runtime.\n\n\nREMEMBER:\n=========\n\nParent reference → Child object\n\n        ↓\n\nCan access Parent-level members\n        +\nOverridden methods execute according to actual Child object\n\n        ↓\n\nCannot directly access Child-specific members.",
